# (id,name,latin,synonyms,system,layer,region,parentId,location,description,function,clinical)
ORG=[
('duodenum','Duodenum','Duodenum',[],'digestive','organs','abdomen','small-intestine','Upper abdomen, a C-shaped loop around the head of the pancreas.','The first and shortest part of the small intestine (about 25 cm). It receives acid food from the stomach, bile from the gallbladder and enzymes from the pancreas.','Neutralises stomach acid, mixes food with bile and pancreatic juice and starts the absorption of iron and calcium.','Duodenal ulcers are common and are mostly caused by Helicobacter pylori infection or anti-inflammatory drugs. A bleeding ulcer can erode the gastroduodenal artery. A tumour of the head of the pancreas can block the duodenal loop or the bile duct.'),
('jejunum','Jejunum','Jejunum',[],'digestive','organs','abdomen','small-intestine','Upper left part of the middle abdomen.','The middle part of the small intestine (about 2.5 m). Its wall has tall circular folds, so it looks feathery inside and has a rich blood supply.','The main site of absorption of sugars, amino acids, fats and vitamins.','Coeliac disease flattens the folds of the jejunum and causes poor absorption. Crohn disease may inflame it. Removing a long segment can cause short bowel syndrome.'),
('ileum','Ileum','Ileum',[],'digestive','organs','abdomen','small-intestine','Lower right part of the middle abdomen, ending at the caecum.','The last and longest part of the small intestine (about 3.5 m). It has Peyer patches (lymphoid tissue) and ends at the ileocaecal valve.','Absorbs vitamin B12 and bile salts and the last of the nutrients, and keeps back large bowel bacteria through the ileocaecal valve.','Crohn disease commonly affects the terminal ileum. A Meckel diverticulum is a leftover pouch 60 cm before the end that can bleed or inflame like appendicitis. Removal of the ileum causes vitamin B12 deficiency.'),
('appendix','Appendix','Appendix vermiformis',['vermiform appendix'],'digestive','organs','abdomen','large-intestine','Lower right abdomen, attached to the caecum just below the ileocaecal valve.','A narrow blind-ended tube about 9 cm long with a lot of lymphoid tissue in its wall. Its tip can lie in several positions.','Probably helps immune defence and may store helpful gut bacteria.','Appendicitis begins with vague central abdominal pain that moves to the lower right (McBurney point) with fever and vomiting. It needs urgent assessment because a burst appendix causes peritonitis.'),
('costal-cartilages','Costal cartilages','Cartilagines costales',[],'skeletal','bones','thorax',None,'Between the front ends of the ribs and the breastbone.','Bars of hyaline cartilage that join the upper seven ribs to the sternum. They are drawn here in pale blue-white.','Make the chest wall springy, so the ribs can move for breathing and absorb blows.','Costochondritis (inflamed cartilage joints) causes chest wall pain that is tender to touch. Cartilage turns to bone with age, making the chest stiffer. They can fracture in CPR.'),
('intervertebral-discs','Intervertebral discs','Disci intervertebrales',['spinal discs'],'skeletal','bones','back',None,'Between the bodies of adjacent vertebrae, from the second cervical vertebra to the sacrum.','Pads with a tough outer ring (annulus fibrosus) around a soft gel centre (nucleus pulposus). They make up about a quarter of the length of the spine.','Absorb shocks, allow bending and twisting and keep the gaps for the spinal nerves open.','A slipped (herniated) disc pushes the gel through the ring, most often at L4-L5 or L5-S1, and presses on a nerve root, causing sciatica (pain down the leg) or numbness. Discs dry out and thin with age.'),
('mandible','Mandible','Mandibula',['lower jaw','jawbone'],'skeletal','bones','head','skull','The lower jaw, hinged to the temporal bone just in front of the ear.','The only movable bone of the skull. A horizontal body carries the lower teeth; two vertical rami end in the condyle (joint) and the coronoid process (muscle attachment). The mental foramen and the mandibular canal carry the inferior alveolar nerve and vessels.','Chewing, speech, and opening and closing the mouth at the temporomandibular joint. Muscles of mastication (masseter, temporalis, pterygoids) attach to it.','Fractures are often double (for example the body and the opposite angle) and the teeth show whether the bite is shifted. Dislocation of the temporomandibular joint locks the mouth open. The inferior alveolar nerve can be injured in wisdom tooth surgery, causing numbness of the lower lip.'),
('teeth','Teeth','Dentes',['dentition'],'skeletal','bones','head','skull','Set in the upper jaw (maxilla) and the lower jaw (mandible).','An adult has 32 permanent teeth: 8 incisors, 4 canines, 8 premolars and 12 molars. Each tooth has an enamel-covered crown, a root in the bony socket and a pulp cavity with nerves and vessels inside dentine.','Bite, cut and grind food, help speech and shape the face.','Dental caries (decay) is the commonest disease worldwide. Untreated, it spreads to the pulp and causes abscess. Gum disease (periodontitis) loosens teeth. Impacted wisdom teeth are common reasons for surgery.'),
('hyoid-bone','Hyoid bone','Os hyoideum',[],'skeletal','bones','neck','skull','In the front of the neck at the level of C3, above the thyroid cartilage, below the mandible.','A small U-shaped bone that does not join any other bone; it is held by muscles and ligaments. It has a body and greater and lesser horns.','Anchors the tongue and the muscles of the floor of the mouth and the larynx, and moves up during swallowing.','A fractured hyoid in an adult is a classic sign of strangulation. It is very rarely injured otherwise.'),
]
VES=[
('renal-artery','Renal artery','Arteria renalis',[],'cardiovascular','vessels','abdomen','From the abdominal aorta just below the superior mesenteric artery to the kidney hilum.','A large paired artery. The right one passes behind the inferior vena cava. Each divides into segmental arteries inside the kidney.','Supplies the kidney with about a fifth of the output of the heart for filtering blood.','Narrowing (renal artery stenosis) causes hard-to-treat high blood pressure. A blocked or torn artery can destroy the kidney. In kidney donation the artery is cut and joined to the recipient vessels.'),
('renal-vein','Renal vein','Vena renalis',[],'cardiovascular','vessels','abdomen','From the kidney hilum to the inferior vena cava.','The left vein is longer and passes in front of the aorta; it also receives the left gonadal and adrenal veins.','Drains filtered blood from the kidney into the inferior vena cava.','A kidney cancer can grow into the renal vein and along the vena cava. A left varicocele may occur because the left testicular vein drains into the left renal vein.'),
('coeliac-trunk','Coeliac trunk and branches','Truncus coeliacus',['celiac artery'],'cardiovascular','vessels','abdomen','Arises from the aorta at the level of T12, just under the diaphragm.','A short trunk that splits into the left gastric, splenic and common hepatic arteries. They are drawn together here.','Supplies the foregut: the lower oesophagus, stomach, liver, gallbladder, spleen, pancreas and the upper duodenum.','A bleeding gastric ulcer may involve the left gastric artery. A splenic artery aneurysm can rupture in pregnancy. Compression by the diaphragm can cause median arcuate ligament syndrome with pain after eating.'),
('superior-mesenteric-artery','Superior mesenteric artery','Arteria mesenterica superior',['SMA'],'cardiovascular','vessels','abdomen','Arises from the aorta at L1, behind the pancreas, and runs down in the root of the mesentery.','Supplies the midgut: the lower duodenum, jejunum, ileum, caecum, appendix, ascending colon and most of the transverse colon.','Carries blood to most of the small bowel and the right colon.','Sudden blockage (an embolus) causes severe abdominal pain out of proportion to the findings. This is a surgical emergency, as the bowel dies within hours (mesenteric ischaemia).'),
('inferior-mesenteric-artery','Inferior mesenteric artery','Arteria mesenterica inferior',['IMA'],'cardiovascular','vessels','abdomen','Arises from the aorta at L3 and runs down on the left.','Supplies the hindgut: the left third of the transverse colon, the descending and sigmoid colon and the upper rectum.','Carries blood to the left colon and upper rectum.','It is tied during surgery for aortic aneurysm or colon cancer. The left colon near the splenic flexure is vulnerable to ischaemic colitis because the supply from two arteries meets there.'),
('superior-mesenteric-vein','Superior mesenteric vein','Vena mesenterica superior',['SMV'],'cardiovascular','vessels','abdomen','Runs beside the superior mesenteric artery on its right side.','Drains the small intestine, caecum, ascending colon and transverse colon, and joins the splenic vein behind the neck of the pancreas to form the portal vein.','Carries nutrient-rich blood from the gut to the liver.','Thrombosis of this vein causes bowel congestion and pain. A pancreatic cancer can grow around it and make it impossible to remove.'),
('splenic-vein','Splenic vein','Vena splenica',[],'cardiovascular','vessels','abdomen','Runs from the spleen along the back of the pancreas.','Collects blood from the spleen, the stomach and the pancreas and joins the superior mesenteric vein to form the portal vein.','Drains the spleen and part of the stomach and pancreas towards the liver.','In pancreatitis or pancreatic cancer the splenic vein can clot, causing sectoral (left-sided) portal hypertension with gastric varices.'),
('internal-iliac-artery','Internal iliac artery','Arteria iliaca interna',['hypogastric artery'],'cardiovascular','vessels','pelvis','Runs from the division of the common iliac artery down into the pelvis.','Supplies the pelvic organs, the gluteal muscles, the inner thigh and the perineum through its anterior and posterior divisions.','Carries blood to the bladder, rectum, uterus or prostate, the gluteal region and the genitals.','Tied or blocked to stop severe bleeding after childbirth or pelvic fracture. In men, narrowing can cause erectile problems and buttock pain on walking.'),
('external-iliac-artery','External iliac artery','Arteria iliaca externa',[],'cardiovascular','vessels','pelvis','Runs along the pelvic brim from the common iliac division to the inguinal ligament.','Becomes the femoral artery as it passes under the inguinal ligament. It gives the inferior epigastric and deep circumflex iliac branches.','Carries the main blood supply to the leg.','Atherosclerosis here causes pain in the thigh and buttock when walking (claudication). It is the artery used for leg access in some heart and aorta procedures.'),
('iliac-veins','Iliac veins','Venae iliacae',[],'cardiovascular','vessels','pelvis','Beside the matching iliac arteries; the two common iliac veins join to form the inferior vena cava at L5.','The external, internal and common iliac veins collect blood from the leg and pelvis.','Return blood from the pelvic organs and the leg to the inferior vena cava.','The left common iliac vein is crossed by the right common iliac artery, and compression there (May-Thurner syndrome) can cause left leg deep vein thrombosis.'),
('subclavian-artery','Subclavian artery','Arteria subclavia',[],'cardiovascular','vessels','thorax','Arches over the first rib behind the collarbone.','The right arises from the brachiocephalic trunk and the left directly from the aortic arch. It gives off the vertebral artery, internal thoracic artery and thyrocervical trunk and becomes the axillary artery.','Supplies the arm, the neck, part of the brain (through the vertebral artery) and the chest wall.','Blockage before the vertebral artery branch can steal blood from the brain when the arm is used (subclavian steal syndrome), causing dizziness. It can be compressed in thoracic outlet syndrome.'),
('subclavian-vein','Subclavian and brachiocephalic veins','Venae subclaviae et brachiocephalicae',[],'cardiovascular','vessels','thorax','Behind the collarbone and into the upper chest, joining to form the superior vena cava.','The subclavian veins drain the arms and join the internal jugular veins to form the brachiocephalic veins, which unite as the superior vena cava.','Return blood from the arms, head and neck to the right atrium.','A popular site for central venous catheters. Clots after catheters or in thoracic outlet syndrome cause an arm that is swollen and blue.'),
('internal-jugular-vein','Internal jugular vein','Vena jugularis interna',[],'cardiovascular','vessels','neck','Runs down the side of the neck beside the common carotid artery in the carotid sheath.','The main vein of the head and neck. It begins at the jugular foramen as the continuation of the sigmoid sinus and joins the subclavian vein.','Drains the brain, face and neck.','The jugular venous pressure (JVP) is read from its pulsation in the neck and shows heart failure. It is a common route for central venous lines.'),
]
